    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHY THE RATIO IS THE RESULT. 5 of 102 rows, 4.9%. A core that is the whole constraint set is the input restated; the product a commercial MILP solver's IIS sells is the compression, and it is only worth anything if irreducibility is real. Both numbers here are measured -- see the two evidence rows. WHAT THIS IS NOT: it is not a claim that this is the SMALLEST core, or the only one. Deletion-based minimization returns ONE irreducible core; a different deletion order can return a different one, and 'irreducible' means no proper subset of THIS core is infeasible, not that no smaller core exists elsewhere in the model. z3, minimizing independently, happened to return the same five rows. WHY `search-certificate` AND NOT `smt-clausal`. The proposition is a conjunction of two halves with different trust bases: the core's `unsat` carries a re-checked Alethe `lia_generic` certificate (clausal-grade), while irreducibility rests on five models replayed by the IR evaluator (witness-grade). `search-certificate` is the enum's name for a search whose result carries a replayable witness or cover, which is exactly this composite; labelling it `smt-clausal` would advertise a certificate for the half that has witnesses instead. See docs/mathematics-2026-08/diary-infeasibility.md.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "command": "python3 render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
        "epoch": {
          "commit": "733126c0f351f0931f7eaf0b4f5f9f569f3aef44",
          "source": "commit",
          "unix": 1787338967
        },
        "exit_status": 0,
        "generator": "render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
